---
// src/pages/beerserker.astro
// Technical breakdown of the enemy AI built for Beerserker: The Stolen Brew.
//
// SCOPE NOTE: Beerserker is a commercial project, so no source from the studio
// repository is reproduced here. Everything below describes architecture and
// decisions, illustrated with diagrams and editor captures.
//
// MEDIA: archetype clips are muted, looping <video> (public/project-images/
// beerserker/videos/*.mp4) rather than <img>/GIF — Astro's image optimisation
// doesn't process animated GIFs, and a properly encoded MP4 is a fraction of
// the size. The StateTree graph stays a static <img> (PNG).
//
// LAYOUT: editorial long-form — running prose, alternating image/text rows and
// pull-quotes instead of the tech-grid/tech-card pattern used on graphic-engine.astro.
// See src/styles/global.css, "Editorial detail page (beerserker)".

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Beerserker Enemy AI · Technical Breakdown — Fernando Fernández Andueza"
  description="How I built the enemy AI of Beerserker: The Stolen Brew in Unreal Engine 5 with StateTree: four archetypes, from a melee enemy to a tank mini-boss with four attacks."
>
  <div class="container page-container">

    <!-- ── PAGE HEADER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="page-header">
      <a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL} class="back-link">← Back to portfolio</a>

      <div class="ed-header-panel">
        <img
          class="ed-header-capsule"
          src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/beerserker/Capsule.png`}
          alt="Beerserker: The Stolen Brew — Steam capsule artwork"
        />

        <div class="ed-header-text">
          <p class="ed-kicker">Enemy AI · Technical breakdown</p>

          <h1>Beerserker</h1>
          <p class="ed-lede">
           Beerserker is a modern fast-paced arcade game and comical twist. Blinded by the legendary dwarven ale's taste, Ragna aims to retrieve this masterpiece from the grasp of the gods. Will you be able to make your way through a crowded dwarven brewery? Or end up crushed by their ranks?
          </p>

          <div class="card-meta" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <span class="badge badge-shipped"><span class="badge-dot"></span>Early Access on Steam</span>
            <span class="card-engine">Unreal Engine 5 &nbsp;·&nbsp; C++ &nbsp;·&nbsp; StateTree</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── THE BRIEF ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="ed-kicker">The brief</h2>

      <span class="ed-label">The production</span>
      <p class="ed-prose">
        Beerserker: The Stolen Brew is my final-year project at <strong>ESAT</strong>, with
        about 20 people across programming, art, design, music and production. It came out on
        Steam in Early Access on <strong>7 July 2026</strong> and is free.
      </p>

      <span class="ed-label">My ownership</span>
      <p class="ed-prose">
        I worked mostly on the AI. I implemented the four enemy archetypes with <strong>StateTree</strong>,
        in C++ and Blueprints, with custom tasks and conditions, and
        used <strong>EQS</strong> to query the environment. I also built a templated object pool
        as a plugin (for enemies, projectiles, decals and particles) and the enemy spawners, and
        I was in charge of configuring and uploading the builds to Steam. The team worked
        collaboratively in Perforce.
      </p>

      <figure class="tech-figure">
        <div class="video-wrapper">
          <iframe
            src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/2bR2OxafTjQ?si=HfbHE73XUxKJRXTU"
            title="Beerserker: The Stolen Brew — Trailer"
            allow="encrypted-media; picture-in-picture"
            loading="lazy"
            allowfullscreen></iframe>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Beerserker: The Stolen Brew — trailer.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- ── THE FOUR ARCHETYPES ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="ed-kicker">The four archetypes</h2>

      <div class="ed-row">
        <div class="ed-num">01</div>
        <div class="ed-media">
          <figure class="tech-figure">
            <video
              autoplay
              loop
              muted
              playsinline
              aria-label="Melee enemy moving towards the player and hitting them"
            >
              <source src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/beerserker/videos/Melee.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
            <figcaption>Moving towards the player and hitting them.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="ed-body">
          <h3>Melee</h3>
          <div class="ed-rule"></div>
          <p>
            It moves towards the player until the player is in range, then hits them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ed-row ed-row--rev">
        <div class="ed-num">02</div>
        <div class="ed-media">
          <figure class="tech-figure">
            <video
              autoplay
              loop
              muted
              playsinline
              aria-label="Ranged enemy retreating to a distant place and shooting at the player"
            >
              <source src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/beerserker/videos/Ranged.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
            <figcaption>Retreating to a distant place and shooting.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="ed-body">
          <h3>Ranged</h3>
          <div class="ed-rule ed-rule--purple"></div>
          <p>
            It gets in range and shoots. When the player is too close, it looks for a distant
            place to retreat to and shoot from.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ed-row">
        <div class="ed-num">03</div>
        <div class="ed-media">
          <figure class="tech-figure">
            <video
              autoplay
              loop
              muted
              playsinline
              aria-label="Explosive enemy blinking faster as its detonation timer runs down"
            >
              <source src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/beerserker/videos/Bomb.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
            <figcaption>The blink telegraph accelerating toward detonation.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="ed-body">
          <h3>Explosive</h3>
          <div class="ed-rule ed-rule--amber"></div>
          <p>
            A red blink speeds up as its detonation timer runs down, so the player can read how
            much time is left at a glance. It detonates on a timer if it never reaches the
            player, after a short commit animation if it does, or on the spot if it is killed
            first — the intended counter is killing it at range before either happens.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ed-row ed-row--rev">
        <div class="ed-num">04</div>
        <div class="ed-media">
          <figure class="tech-figure">
            <video
              autoplay
              loop
              muted
              playsinline
              aria-label="Tank mini-boss cycling between its four attack patterns"
            >
              <source src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/beerserker/videos/Tank.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
            <figcaption>Choosing between four attack patterns.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="ed-body">
          <h3>Tank mini-boss</h3>
          <div class="ed-rule ed-rule--green"></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Four attacks:</strong> a ranged honey throw, a ranged dwarf throw, a melee
            sweep that leaves a honey hazard on the ground, and a melee area attack. The two
            ranged attacks are each chosen independently by their own cooldown and range check;
            the two melee attacks share a cooldown and are chosen by weight, so the fight
            doesn't fall into repeating the same attack. Throwing the dwarf is its own short
            sequence — call it over, wait for it to arrive, confirm it's still alive, grab it,
            throw it — falling back to a different attack if the dwarf isn't available.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── WHY STATETREE, NOT BEHAVIOR TREE ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="ed-kicker">Why StateTree, not Behavior Tree</h2>

      <p class="ed-lede ed-lede-drop">
        We chose <strong>StateTree</strong> because Epic is adopting it as a standard, and
        because we were forcing Behavior Trees to behave like state machines. For that, we
        used a state machine directly: a StateTree.
      </p>

      <figure class="tech-figure">
        <img
          src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/beerserker/statetree-graph.png`}
          alt="Annotated StateTree graph for one of the enemy archetypes"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          One of the archetypes' StateTree graphs, annotated.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- ── DESIGN DECISIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="ed-kicker">Design decisions</h2>

      <div class="ed-list">
        <div class="ed-item">
          <span class="ed-item-index">01</span>
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Many enemies on screen all the time</h3>
            <p>
              I programmed a generic object pool as a plugin, so it is modular and can be taken
              to other projects, because of the nature of the game: many enemies on screen all
              the time, plus particles, decals and projectiles. When an enemy goes back to the
              pool, its StateTree stops on the next frame, and when it is reused it starts again
              from the beginning.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ed-item">
          <span class="ed-item-index">02</span>
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Where the ranged enemy retreats to</h3>
            <p>
              The enemy retreats to a safe place, chosen with an <strong>EQS</strong> query by
              its distance to the player and its line of sight to them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ed-item">
          <span class="ed-item-index">03</span>
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Which attack the tank picks</h3>
            <p>
              Each of the tank's melee attacks starts with a weight of 1, and every time it uses
              one, that attack's weight goes up. The less an attack has been used, the more likely
              it is to come up, so the tank doesn't keep repeating the same one.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ed-item">
          <span class="ed-item-index">04</span>
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Which dwarf the tank throws</h3>
            <p>
              When the tank calls a dwarf, it looks for the dwarves within a configurable radius,
              checks with a line trace that it can see them, and picks the closest one. The
              selection is triggered by an AnimNotifyState at the point in the animation where
              the tank calls the dwarf.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ed-item">
          <span class="ed-item-index">05</span>
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Stun through StateTree events</h3>
            <p>
              When the player hits an enemy with an attack that stuns, the enemy receives
              the <code>StateTree.Stun</code> event, which takes it into the stun state. When the
              animation ends, an anim notify sends <code>StateTree.StunFinish</code> to leave it.
              The tank can't be stunned.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ed-item">
          <span class="ed-item-index">06</span>
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Exposed attributes and varied melee attacks</h3>
            <p>
              The enemies' attributes are exposed in the editor so they can be modified: attack
              range, cooldowns, detection radius or honey distances. Each attack's cooldown is set
              by GameplayTag and checked by a custom StateTree condition. In the melee enemies,
              attacks are a list of animations: adding a new one is enough for the enemy to use
              it, so the game doesn't feel so repetitive.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── LINKS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="ed-kicker">The game</h2>
      <div class="card-links">
        <a
          href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/4522440/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="btn-primary"
        >
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
            <path
              d="M12.695 0C8.772 0 5.314 2.578 4.05 6.148l6.624 2.777c.666-.57 1.55-.923 2.526-.923 2.137 0 3.872 1.735 3.872 3.872 0 .976-.353 1.86-.923 2.527l2.777 6.624C21.422 18.686 24 15.228 24 11.305 24 5.06 18.94 0 12.695 0zM5.997 15.17c-.426.668-.667 1.458-.667 2.304 0 2.395 1.942 4.336 4.337 4.336.846 0 1.636-.24 2.304-.666l-5.974-5.974z"
            ></path>
          </svg>
          Play on Steam
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /.container -->

</Layout>
